   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="address">
                    <ab>
                        <address>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI_28">42 rue Galilée</name>
                            </addrLine>                                                                                                        	<addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI">Paris</name> XVI</addrLine>
                        </address>
                    </ab>
                </div>
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>Dear Budgen: <name type="person" key="weav76">Miss Weaver</name> likes the <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">picture</seg>. What do I owe for the frame? Let me know on what <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">date</seg> you are likely to pass here as we might be away for a day or so. They should have sent you galleys in duplicate. I forgot <name type="person" key="bera68">Felix Beran</name>, the Viennese poet, in Z’ch − the only writer I knew there. You remember his poem I translated, ending:</p>
                    <lg>        	<l>
                            <foreign xml:lang="deu">
                                <seg xml:id="seg3" type="note-anchor">Wer wird nun küssen</seg>
                            </foreign>
                        </l>        	<l>
                            <foreign xml:lang="deu">
                                <choice>
                                    <sic>Mein</sic>
                                    <corr>Meinen</corr>
                                </choice> weissen Leib</foreign> (etc)</l>
                    </lg>
                    <p>
                        <hi rend="underline">Re</hi> the <seg xml:id="seg4" type="note-anchor">explosion</seg> in Phoenix Park what about W.i.P.?</p>
                    <lg>        	<l>
                            <seg xml:id="seg5" type="note-anchor">Have you heard of one Humpty Dumpty</seg>
                        </l>        	<l>How he fell with a roll &amp; a rumble</l>        	<l>And lay low like Lord All of a crumple</l>        	<l>By the butt of the Magazine Wall?</l>
                    </lg>
                    <p>(This is on the road to Chapelizod)</p>
                    <p>cf “<seg xml:id="seg6" type="note-anchor">Badalgaraghtakamminar etc</seg>”</p>
                    <p>Transition no 1., 1927</p>        	<p>The lens of my glass is unfixed from the heat so that I can’t see well and <name type="person" key="joyc07">Lucia</name>’s pen is as capricious as herself.</p>
                    <closer>        	<salute>sincerely yours</salute>                    	<signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">James Joyce</name>
                        </signed>
                        <dateline>
                            <date when="1933-09-05">5.ix.933</date>
                        </dateline>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">The picture was a birthday gift for <name type="person" key="weav76">Weaver</name>, mentioned in JJ's <rs type="letter" key="L_70347">letter to Budgen of 24 August 1933</rs> (<name type="work">LIII</name> 283, where the date is mistakenly given as 28 August).</note>
                <note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">Budgen visited JJ for a day or two in Paris in early September while on his way to Ascona. The visit was an opportunity for JJ, then reading galley proofs for <name type="work">JJMU</name>, to offer Budgen suggestions for addition and revision.</note>
                <note xml:id="note3" target="#seg3" type="commentary">These two lines conclude <name type="person" key="bera68">Beran</name>'s poem “Des Weibes Klage”, discussed by Budgen in <name type="work">JJMU</name> 12-13.</note>
                <note xml:id="note4" target="#seg4" type="commentary">The explosion occurred on 4 September: “Phoenix Park Explosion. Magazine Wall Damaged”, <name type="work">Irish Times</name> (5 September 1933): 7.</note>
                <note xml:id="note5" target="#seg5" type="commentary">These lines appear in slightly different form in “Continuation of a Work in Progress”, <name type="work">transition</name> no. 2 (May 1927), 104.</note>
                <note xml:id="note6" target="#seg6" type="commentary">With a few different letters, this was the beginning of the thunderword in “Opening Pages of a Work in Progress”, <name type="work">transition</name> no. 1 (April 1927), 9.</note>
            </div>
      </body>
